  "opening": "In Chapter 1, we saw that a computation is a sequence of transitions between configurations, and that reversing a step is impossible whenever distinct inputs merge into the same output. How can we build general-purpose computations out of elementary steps on bits without losing information?",
  "opening_continuation": "We first represent data and computational tasks using bits, Boolean functions, and logic circuits. We then examine the physical cost of erasing information and show how to compute any Boolean function using reversible gates.",
  "intro_continuation": "For example, an operation that outputs \\(1\\) only when two input bits are both \\(1\\) gives the output \\(0\\) for three different input pairs: \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\). Once only \\(0\\) remains, there is no way to tell which input pair produced it. Before we can make such steps reversible, we first need to see how data is encoded in bits and how functions on bits are built from elementary operations."
